  "network": {
   "confidence": "datasheet",
   "note": "Data sheet: \"2 x 10G SFP+ Fiber and 2 x 1000 Base-T Ethernet\". The front photograph prints NET1 SFP+ over NET2 SFP+ on two stacked cages and NET3 over NET4 on two stacked RJ45 jacks, each jack with a green and an amber lamp window."
  },
  "power": {
   "confidence": "datasheet",
   "note": "Data sheet: dual socket universal IEC C14 inlet, 100-240 V AC 50/60 Hz; every appliance ships with two C14 power cables. The data sheet gives no power consumption for the CM8116-10G-USB (it gives CM8148-10G, CM8196-10G, CM8148-10G-USB and CM8148-10G-C), so none is written. The rear prints the numbers 1 and 2 with a plug symbol under each inlet."
  },
  "rear-layout": {
   "confidence": "estimated",
   "note": "Method as the front: the CM8116-10G-USB rear photograph is 1961 px wide for 440 mm (4.46 px/mm) and 191 px high for 44 mm (4.34 px/mm). The eight USB-C ports (pitch 16.05 mm, 71.5 px), the two C14 inlets and the printing are read off it, good to about 1 mm across and 1.5 mm down. The inlet openings in the photograph are smaller than the library's C14 part; the part is drawn as it is."
  },
  "serial": {
   "confidence": "datasheet",
   "note": "Data sheet: CM8116-10G has 16 RJ45 RS-232 serial ports with a software selectable \"Straight or Rollover\" pinout, 50 to 230,400 bps. The pinout drawing prints, for the device ports, X1 \"Cisco Rolled\" (1 RTS, 2 DTR, 3 TxD, 4 GND, 5 CTS, 6 RxD, 7 DCD, 8 DSR) and X2 \"Cisco Straight\" (1 CTS, 2 DSR DCD, 3 RxD, 4 GND, 5 not connected, 6 TxD, 7 DTR, 8 RTS), and for the local console X1 with pins 2, 5 and 7 not connected. The photograph numbers the ports 1, 3 to 15 over 2, 4 to 16, with SERIAL printed between 7 and 9."
  },
  "serial-role": {
   "confidence": "datasheet",
   "note": "The serial ports are console server ports. The CM8100-10G family data sheet, p.1, says of the family (not of this model alone): \"Secure, remote, access to manage up to 96 serial devices\". It also lists \"IP alias for serial port console connections\" under Console Management. The RJ45 console on the front is the local console (data sheet, p.1, \"Console Port\")."
  },
